## Two sets of six bodies.
##
## STAUNTON is the MIT-licensed set the project has always used: five turned
## bodies plus a knight generated by tools/build_knight.py. It is deliberately
## plain - every bit of its carving is added at runtime by _ornate_mesh().
##
## MEDIEVAL is the hand-sculpted set the author uploaded. It arrives already
## carved - the pawns have a rough-hewn foot, the rook has bitten merlons, the
## bishop's mitre is chiselled and the king is a robed figure holding a staff -
## so its trim is limited to a pair of gold rules at the base and nothing else;
## bolting the Staunton crowns onto it would be two crowns on one head.
##
## COURT is a set of court figures rather than turned pieces: a guard with a
## spear and a shield for the pawn, a rearing horse for the knight, an elephant
## for the bishop, a crowned noblewoman for the queen and a robed king holding a
## sceptre, all standing on engraved plinths. It replaces the old Persian set,
## which was the same carved-lathe family as MEDIEVAL and differed only in
## colour - three sets of which two were the same silhouette is exactly the
## repetition the style screen was trimmed to avoid.
##
## All three sets are OBJ with no UVs and no normals; _piece_mesh() projects and
## smooths them, and tools/prepare_sculpt_set.py is what put the second set on
## this project's height ladder in the first place.
##
## These are paths rather than preloads on purpose. A preload resolves when the
## script itself is parsed, so six preloads per set meant all eighteen bodies -
## about 4 MB of vertex data - were resident before the first frame, whether the
## player ever chose that set or not. On a phone that is both startup time and
## memory the game never gets back. _piece_models() loads the set that is
## actually being worn and keeps it; the other two are only touched if the
## player picks them, and they stay loaded once they have been.
